Q: I&#39;m trying to recover images from a memory card, but most of the images I&#39;m getting are corrupted. Can anybody tell me why this is happening? A: <p>In attempting to analyze the above block of code, I zeroed in on the test for a header.</p>
<pre><code>if (buffer1[0] == 0xff &amp;&amp; buffer1[1] == 0xd8 &amp;&amp; buffer1[2] == 0xff &amp;&amp; buffer1[3]/0x10==0x0e)
</code></pre>
<p>In the last part of the test, it appears that you are dividing the value stored in buffer[3] by &quot;16&quot; and then comparing that value to &quot;0x0e&quot;, which is not going to provide you with the desired true/false examination.  In reviewing tutorials on JPEG formats, the usual test utilizes a bitwise &quot;and&quot; process and then testing those results as follows.</p>
<pre><code>if (buffer1[0] == 0xff &amp;&amp; buffer1[1] == 0xd8 &amp;&amp; buffer1[2] == 0xff &amp;&amp; (buffer1[3] &amp; 0xf0) == 0xe0)
</code></pre>
<p>With that, I attempted to refactor the while loops to get the code to behave, and I could never get to a point of success.  Therefore, utilizing analysis and answers from previous like minded issue entries, following is a refactored version of the code, utilizing as many of the same variable names as possible.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    unsigned char buffer1[512];                     /* Buffer to receive fread input                    */
    int i = 0;
    char gp[20];                                    /* Character array to contain individual file names */

    if (argc != 2)                                  /* Ensure proper number of parameters               */
    {
        printf(&quot;Improper number of parameters!\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    FILE *card = fopen(argv[1], &quot;rb&quot;);              /* Usually open these files in binary mode          */

    if (card == NULL)                               /* Validating entered file name exists              */
    {
        printf(&quot;File wasn't found\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    FILE *f = NULL;                                 /* File pointer for outputted image file(s)         */

    while (fread(buffer1, sizeof(buffer1), sizeof(unsigned char), card) == 1)
    {
        if (buffer1[0] == 0xff &amp;&amp; buffer1[1] == 0xd8 &amp;&amp; buffer1[2] == 0xff &amp;&amp; (buffer1[3] &amp; 0xf0) == 0xe0)
        {
            if (f != NULL)                          /* Close previous image file                        */
                fclose(f);

            sprintf(gp, &quot;Img%03d.jpg&quot;, i + 1);      /* Use sprintf to construct name for image file     */

            f = fopen(gp, &quot;wb&quot;);                    /* Usual and customary is a binary file             */
            if (f == NULL)
            {
                printf(&quot;Couldn't open file\n&quot;);
                return 1;
            }
            i++;
        }

        if (f != NULL)
            fwrite(buffer1, sizeof(buffer1), sizeof(unsigned char), f);
    }

    if (f != NULL)
        fclose(f);

    fclose(card);

}
</code></pre>
<p>Per the good comments, the reading and looping does not rely on the &quot;!feof(card)&quot; condition.  Also, when reading from and writing to image files, it is usual and customary to open the files for binary usage (e.g. &quot;rb&quot; and &quot;wb&quot;).</p>
<p>The main things to take away here would be to delve some more into C tutorials as they pertain to opening and closing files, reading and writing to files, bitwise manipulation, and also more study of the structures of image files.</p>

Q: TypeError: Cannot read property startsWith of undefined A: <p>search for startsWith on your app</p>
<p>Always **guard against undefined</p>
<p>example:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>//instead of using it like this
I18n.locale = Localization.locale.startsWith(&quot;ar&quot;) ? &quot;ar&quot; : &quot;en&quot;;
//add this line:
typeof Localization.locale === &quot;string&quot; &amp;&amp;
//so it will be like this :
I18n.locale =
  typeof Localization.locale === &quot;string&quot; &amp;&amp;
  Localization.locale.startsWith(&quot;ar&quot;)
    ? &quot;ar&quot;
    : &quot;en&quot;;

//do same for urls
</code></pre>
